[center][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/fo4-chemistry@63e6741af3a8ff2f200de390be9a2fe3df6145c5/docs/img/divider.png[/img][/center]

[center][b]The Commonwealth family: made to work together[/b]
[url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fallout4/mods/109219]Rapport[/url]: runs the scenes · [b]Chemistry[/b] (this page): NPCs find each other · [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fallout4/mods/109433]Overture[/url]: you talk someone into it · [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fallout4/mods/109435]Anatomy[/url]: working genitals, arousal and contact · [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fallout4/mods/109434]Anatomy Engine[/url]: the physics behind them · [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fallout4/mods/109439]Silhouette[/url]: everyone gets a body of their own · [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/games/fallout4/collections/dmt85e]Ivy's Panties[/url]: all of it, installed for you[/center]

[size=5][b][color=#3fd6bd]In short[/color][/b][/size]

[b]What you get[/b]
[list]
[*]NPCs who know each other, like spouses, family, friends and co-workers, start scenes together by themselves.[/*]
[*]Pairs are likelier to get together at home or in their own faction's building.[/*]
[*]Married NPCs mostly stay faithful, each by their own nature.[/*]
[*]Your lover (if Overture made someone your lover) counts as taken when anybody else comes along.[/*]
[*]Most of the time nothing happens. Scenes are rare on purpose.[/*]
[/list][b]What you need[/b]: [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fallout4/mods/109219]Rapport[/url] 0.2.1 or newer, loaded before Chemistry.
[b]How it goes[/b]: install Rapport, then Chemistry. Play. Details in [b]Before you play[/b], below.

[size=5][b][color=#3fd6bd]What it is[/color][/b][/size]

Chemistry decides [i]when[/i] two NPCs get together, and what kind of scene it is. That is all it does. Rapport on its own doesn't start scenes: Chemistry is what makes NPCs pair up. [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fallout4/mods/109219]Rapport[/url] does everything you see and hear: the voices, the faces, the bystanders and the Narrator. Chemistry is two Papyrus scripts and one small plugin. It has no DLL, no textures and no animations of its own.

Every pair of NPCs nearby is weighed on how well they know each other, whose place it is, who is watching and whether one of them is spoken for. Most of the time the answer is no.

[size=5][b][color=#3fd6bd]What you get[/color][/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Couples, not coincidences.[/b] People who already know each other, such as spouses, family, friends and co-workers, are far likelier to get together. Every scene they share brings them closer.[/*]
[*][b]Their own place.[/b] A pair indoors in one of their own homes, or in their faction's building, is much likelier. At home it is unhurried, whoever walks through.[/*]
[*][b]Personalities matter.[/b] Two of a kind get on. The romantic one and the crude one clash. The crude one likes an audience, and the shy one hates one.[/*]
[*][b]Faithfulness.[/b] Married and courting NPCs mostly stay true, each by their own nature. A devoted spouse almost never strays, and a restless one might. If they do, it is remembered as an affair.[/*]
[*][b]Your lover is spoken for.[/b] If Overture has made someone your lover, they are treated as taken when anybody else comes along.[/*]
[*][b]Nobody runs wild.[/b] Each person rests a day after a scene. Anyone AAF keeps refusing is left alone for longer each time.[/*]
[/list][center][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/fo4-chemistry@63e6741af3a8ff2f200de390be9a2fe3df6145c5/docs/img/score.jpg[/img][/center]

[center][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/fo4-chemistry@63e6741af3a8ff2f200de390be9a2fe3df6145c5/docs/img/bond.jpg[/img][/center]

A bond starts from what the game already knows about the two of them. A married couple starts at 0.80 before their first scene. Every scene together closes part of the distance left.

[size=5][b][color=#3fd6bd]How it works[/color][/b][/size]

Every 30 seconds or so, Chemistry looks at the pairs Rapport has ranked and adds what it knows about them. The best pair that clears the bar gets a scene. If nobody clears it, nothing happens, and that is most of the time. The place and the audience then pick which of Rapport's three scenes it is:

[center][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/fo4-chemistry@63e6741af3a8ff2f200de390be9a2fe3df6145c5/docs/img/story.jpg[/img][/center]

When nothing happens, Rapport's Narrator tells you who nearly did and why (MCM, the in-game settings menu: Narrator).

[b]How it works, in detail:[/b] [url=https://www.nexusmods.com/fallout4/articles/6681]Chemistry - under the hood →[/url]

[center][img]https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/ReidenXerx/fo4-chemistry@63e6741af3a8ff2f200de390be9a2fe3df6145c5/docs/img/divider.png[/img][/center]

[size=5][b][color=#3fd6bd]Also in Chemistry[/color][/b][/size]

[list]
[*][b]Your settings always apply.[/b] Chemistry reads its settings through Rapport, because MCM's own store sometimes answered as if nothing was set.[/*]
[*][b]Pairs respect orientation.[/b] Rapport never offers a pair who would not want each other, so neither does Chemistry.[/*]
[*][b]Your own scene comes first.[/b] While Rapport holds its one scene slot for a scene you asked for in Overture, Chemistry waits.[/*]
[*][b]A moved slider can no longer switch autonomy off by accident.[/b][/*]
[*][b]Light plugin.[/b] Chemistry.esp is an ESL, a light plugin that takes no load-order slot. With Rapport 0.2.3 it runs on the Anniversary Edition too.[/*]
[*][b]Servitrons are always willing.[/b] With Anatomy Servitron and Rapport 0.2.19, nothing in Chemistry holds one back; her partner still has their own reasons.[/*]
[/list][size=5][b][color=#3fd6bd]Before you play[/color][/b][/size]

[quote]
[b]Install with Vortex or MO2; manual installs are not supported.[/b]

[b]Chemistry needs Rapport 0.2.1 or newer[/b], loaded before it. With an older Rapport it does nothing and says so once in [font=Courier New]Rapport.log[/font]. Update Rapport and it starts on the next load, with no reinstall. It cannot run without Rapport.

[b]Nothing starts until you leave Vault 111.[/b] No scene and no approach during the game's opening, from character creation to the vault door. Alternate starts and MS Skip Prewar Sanctuary work as expected. Using an unusual alternate start that never finishes the opening? In MCM, first page, press [i]Start now[/i]. [i]Start now[/i] needs Rapport 0.2.2.

[b]MCM is optional.[/b] Without it, the defaults apply, and they are what is described above.[/quote][size=5][b][color=#3fd6bd]Incompatibilities[/color][/b][/size]

Known conflicts, from real reports and the code. Found another one? Tell us in the Bugs tab.
[list]
[*][b]None known. Chemistry shares Rapport's rules[/b]: see Rapport's list.[/*]
[/list][size=5][b][color=#3fd6bd]FAQ[/color][/b][/size]

[b]Do I need AAF?[/b]
[spoiler]Every scene plays on AAF, and Rapport carries the scenes out. Chemistry itself needs Rapport. See Rapport's page for the rest.[/spoiler]

[b]Will scenes happen in front of me?[/b]
[spoiler]They can. Whether you are nearby is deliberately not a factor, so the world does not stop because you walked in. The crowd around them is a factor, and you do not count as the crowd.[/spoiler]

[b]Too many scenes, or too few?[/b]
[spoiler]Raise or lower [i]Score needed[/i] (default 0.90) and [i]Rest per person[/i] (default 24 game hours) in MCM. There is no global rate limit: a busy settlement has more scenes simply because it has more pairs.[/spoiler]

[b]Can I make it decide right now?[/b]
[spoiler]Yes, from MCM's Debug page or two hotkeys. REAL runs an ordinary decision with every rule. FORCED takes the best pair regardless of the bar and the rest time. Both still wait for a running scene.[/spoiler]

[b]Where is every setting explained?[/b]
[spoiler]In the sticky post, with every MCM control and what it does.[/spoiler]

